\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Induced structures and the discrete-to-continuum problem}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-15}

\p{This opening note fixes the evidence boundary for Formulating Discrete Induced Physics (FDIP). It begins with a scoped literature survey, before introducing a new algebraic or geometric construction. The question is precise: when a discrete model is said to \em{induce} gauge, metric, or continuum structure, what carrier, action, limit, and observable establish that claim?}

\subtree{
  \title{Four primary cases}

  \p{Aoki, Gocksch, and Shen study the SU(2) Kazakov--Migdal model proposed as ``induced QCD'' in \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9208029}. Their numerical study keeps the gauge fields in the simulation so that gauge observables can be measured. They find a first-order transition line ending at a critical point, but also extra local U(1) symmetries; two gauge-field angles decouple, and the continuum limit around that critical point therefore cannot be QCD. This is a useful negative result: a critical point and an induced action do not identify the intended continuum theory.}

  \p{Shamir proposes a different induction mechanism in \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9402109}. Matter fields live on a dynamical random spatial lattice with regular time steps. The model has a diffeomorphism-invariant bilinear scalar action but no pure-gravity action. A finite-energy zero-momentum scalar excitation supplies evidence for a non-trivial vanishing-spacing limit; under an additional assumption about localized low-energy states, quasi-local microscopic averages define an effective curved metric. The metric is therefore an induced effective description under stated state and observable assumptions, not a derivation of general relativity.}

  \p{de Divitiis and collaborators test universality in SU(2) lattice gauge theory in \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9411017}. They compute two non-perturbatively defined running couplings, simulate sequences of lattices with decreasing spacings, and extrapolate to the continuum. Their agreement at the reported precision supports a universality comparison, while their warning about matching renormalized couplings at high energies identifies a surviving interface. A continuum claim needs an indexed family, a declared observable, and a matching or convergence argument.}

  \p{Oriti presents Group Field Theory as a candidate description of fundamentally discrete quantum spacetime in \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0612301}. The proposed research programme treats continuum spacetime as a possible condensed phase and asks how General Relativity could emerge through QFT methods. This supplies a discrete-to-continuum programme, not a unique continuum construction or an established Einstein-dynamics result.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{What the survey establishes}

  \p{Across these cases, induction is a relation between a specified microscopic carrier and a selected effective description. The carrier may be a matrix or lattice model, a dynamical triangulation, or a field-theoretic combinatorial structure. The effective object is not licensed by vocabulary alone: it must be tied to an action or transition rule, a state or path-integral prescription, an observable, and a limit or phase-selection argument.}

  \p{The literature also supplies three independent failure boundaries. Extra symmetries can change the target theory; a metric can be defined only after assumptions about low-energy states and observables; and a continuum comparison can fail at the matching step even when lattice data look universal. FDIP should preserve these as tests rather than smooth them into a single success story.}

  \p{For the calculational baseline, the supplied \citelink{https://cns.gatech.edu/~predrag/courses/PHYS-6124-12/lectQM.pdf} and \citelink{https://indico.global/event/14222/attachments/61764/119960/lft_notes.pdf}, together with the pedagogical sources \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/0211036} and \citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.02988}, will be surveyed in FDIP-2. They are preparation for a declared lattice vocabulary, not evidence that one finite example realizes the FDIP target.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{FDIP vocabulary admitted by this note}

  \p{The first Forest batch may now use the following terms with their limits made explicit: a \newvocab{microscopic carrier} is the finite or discrete object on which fields and weights are defined; an \newvocab{induced structure} is an effective operator, metric, coupling, or phase obtained from that carrier by a stated construction; a \newvocab{comparison observable} is a normalized quantity whose family can be followed as the spacing or control parameter changes; and a \newvocab{continuum claim} requires an indexed family and a specified limiting or matching procedure.}

  \p{No claim is made here about a finite-group algebra, Clifford or geometric algebra, a background fibration, a topos, an arithmetic carrier, or a Standard-Model/QFT/GR recovery. Those are later candidate branches. Each must first answer an obstruction found in a declared baseline and then pass its own literature survey.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Open interfaces for the next notes}

  \ol{
    \li{What exact lattice or cell carrier, coefficient field, action, boundary condition, measure, and normalized observable will serve as FDIP's reference baseline?}
    \li{Which symmetry or representation data act on that carrier, and what equivariance or invariant is actually proved?}
    \li{Which failed interface justifies admitting a Clifford, gauge, operator, topos, or arithmetic branch?}
    \li{Which indexed family and observable would make a later continuum comparison meaningful?}
  }

  \p{FDIP-1 ends at these questions. FDIP-2 must survey its lattice and discrete-calculus sources before defining the reference substrate; only then may a local propagator, transfer identity, or tree-level witness be introduced.}
}
